// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Writing direction (LTR / RTL).
//
// `<html dir>` is set by TranslationService, and by the no-FOUC script in each
// app's index.html before first paint, so an RTL locale never flashes LTR.
//
// LAYOUT NEEDS NO RULES HERE. Every stylesheet in this system is written with
// CSS logical properties — margin-inline-start, padding-block, inset-inline-end,
// border-start-start-radius, text-align: start — which the browser resolves
// against `dir` on its own. The only physical values that belong in the system
// are `env(safe-area-inset-*)`, and those are physical by definition: a notch is
// on the device's left whichever way the text runs.
//
// So the rule for new code is simply: never write left/right, only start/end.
// What follows covers the two things logical properties genuinely cannot do.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
@layer base {
  // ── 1. Mirroring direction-encoding glyphs ─────────────────────────────────
  // A logical property moves a box; it cannot redraw an icon. A "next" chevron,
  // a reply arrow or an undo curve all point the wrong way in RTL and must be
  // mirrored. Opt-in, deliberately: search, close, calendar, play and every
  // brand mark must NOT flip, so a blanket `[dir='rtl'] svg { … }` would be a
  // bug factory. Tag only the icons that carry direction.
  [dir='rtl'] .flip-inline {
    transform: scaleX(-1);
  }

  // ── 2. Pinning direction for content that is not prose ─────────────────────
  // The bidi algorithm reorders neutral characters (`/ . : - + ( )`) to match
  // the surrounding paragraph, which mangles anything whose order is
  // structural: URLs, file paths, code, version numbers, phone numbers, IBANs.
  // `isolate` also stops the fragment from reordering the text around it.
  //
  // PUT THESE ON AN INLINE ELEMENT — a <span> around the value, not the <td> or
  // <dd> containing it. `direction` also decides how `text-align: start`
  // resolves, so on a block it would drag the whole value to the far edge of its
  // box; on an inline span the block keeps the page's alignment and only the
  // characters inside are reordered, which is what is actually wanted.
  .ltr {
    direction: ltr;
    unicode-bidi: isolate;
  }

  .rtl {
    direction: rtl;
    unicode-bidi: isolate;
  }
}
